\description{
\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#experimental}{\figure{lifecycle-experimental.svg}{options: alt='[Experimental]'}}}{\strong{[Experimental]}}

The total deviation index (TDI) is the value that a proportion \code{p} of the
absolute differences between two methods do not exceed: P(|D| <= TDI) = p
(Lin, 2000). The coverage probability (CP) is the reverse: the proportion of
absolute differences that do not exceed a given value \code{delta}. \code{tdi()}
estimates both from a fitted agreement analysis, with a one-sided upper
confidence bound for the TDI and a lower bound for the CP, and optionally
tests whether the TDI is below \code{delta}.
}
\details{
For differences \code{D ~ N(mu, sigma^2)}, the TDI is computed exactly,
\code{sigma * sqrt(qchisq(p, 1, ncp = (mu / sigma)^2))}, the \code{p} quantile of
\verb{|D|}. Lin's (2000) approximation, \code{qnorm((1 + p) / 2) * sqrt(mu^2 + sigma^2)}, is reported for reference; it overstates the TDI when the bias
is large relative to the SD. The CP of \code{delta} is
\code{pnorm((delta - mu) / sigma) - pnorm((-delta - mu) / sigma)}.

\code{mu} and \code{sigma} are the bias and the SD of a single difference reported by
\code{object}, with their degrees of freedom: for \code{agreement_limit()}, \code{df} and
\code{df_sd} of the \code{loa} table (\code{df_sd} is a Satterthwaite df for replicated
and nested data); for \code{tolerance_limit()}, \code{df} and \code{SD.df}; for
\code{method_vc()}, the \code{df} of the bias and \code{df_sd}. With a bias that depends
on the level (\code{prop_bias = TRUE}), several conditions, or several pairs of
methods, the TDI is computed for every row, and the printed estimate and
bound are those of the row with the largest upper bound.

Bounds:
\itemize{
\item \code{"gpq"}: generalized pivotal quantities (Krishnamoorthy & Mathew, 2009).
The variance of a single difference is split into independent pieces
\code{v_k} with \code{df_k} degrees of freedom, each drawn as
\verb{v_k* = v_k df_k / U_k} with \code{U_k} chi-square on \code{df_k} df, and
\verb{sigma*^2 = sum(v_k*)}. For independent differences there is one piece,
the variance itself. For \code{agreement_limit(data_type = "reps")} the pieces
are the variance of the subject mean differences and the rest of the
within-subject variance of each method; for \code{data_type = "nest"} and
\code{tolerance_limit()} with subjects, the variance of a subject mean and the
remaining within-subject (and nested) variance. The standard error of
the bias depends on the first piece, so \verb{mu* = mu - Z SE sqrt(v_1* / v_1)}. For \code{method_vc()} and other correlation structures, the variance
is drawn on its Satterthwaite df and \verb{mu* = mu - T SE}, with \code{T} on the
df of the bias. The bound for the TDI is the \code{conf.level}
quantile of \verb{TDI(mu*, sigma*)}, the bound for the CP the \code{1 - conf.level} quantile of \verb{CP(delta; mu*, sigma*)}, and the p-value is the
proportion of draws with \verb{TDI(mu*, sigma*) >= delta}.
\item \code{"delta"}: the delta method for \code{log(TDI)} (and \code{logit(CP)}), with
\code{var(mu) = SE^2}, \verb{var(sigma^2) = 2 sigma^4 / nu}, and \code{t} quantiles on
\code{nu} df.
\item \code{"tolerance"}: Escaramis et al. (2010). The TDI is written as
\verb{|mu| + z_q sigma}, where \code{q} solves
\verb{pnorm(z_q) - pnorm(-z_q - 2 |mu| / sigma) = p}, and the bound is the
one-sided \code{conf.level} upper tolerance bound for the \code{q} quantile: the
exact noncentral t bound for independent differences, as in
\code{tolerance_limit(bound_type = "iu")}, or its MOVER bound with subjects.
\code{q} is estimated, so the bound is approximate. TDIagree's version of this
bound counts measurements rather than differences (\code{n} = 2 x subjects,
\code{df} = subjects), which makes it narrower; here the SE of the bias and the
df of the SD of the differences are used. The CP bound is found by
inverting the TDI bound in \code{p}.
}
}
